sec.p:
The sum of the magnitude of the 3D displacement vector, d→i\begin{document}$\vec{d}_{i}$\end{document} moved on the curved embryo surface between each timepoint i, over the total duration, T.


vtotal=1T∑iN‖d→i‖
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle v_{\text {total }}=\frac{1}{T} \sum_{i}^{N}\left\|\vec{d}_{i}\right\|$$\end{document}

The magnitude of the resultant sum of the 3D displacement vector, d→i\begin{document}$\vec{d}_{i}$\end{document}, moved on the curved embryo surface between each timepoint i, over the total duration, T.
vmean=1T‖∑iNdi→‖ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle v_{\text {mean }}=\frac{1}{T}\left\|\sum_{i}^{N} \overrightarrow{d_{i}}\right\|$$\end{document}
It corresponds to measuring the direct curvilinear distance travelled between the initial and final surface coordinates along a track.

The resultant sum of the anteriorwards projection of the 3D displacement vector, d→i\begin{document}$\vec{d}_{i}$\end{document} moved on the curved embryo surface between each timepoint i, over the total duration, T.
vant=1T∑iNd→i⋅eant→ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle v_{a n t}=\frac{1}{T} \sum_{i}^{N} \vec{d}_{i} \cdot \overrightarrow{e_{a n t}}$$\end{document}
where eant→\begin{document}$\overrightarrow{e_{\text {ant }}}$\end{document} denotes the local anteriorwards unit surface vector and ± is the scalar dot product. eant→\begin{document}$\overrightarrow{e_{\text {ant }}}$\end{document} is computed by finding the equivalent 3D surface vectors when the vertical ‘upward’ anterior 2D vector in the unwrapped 2D polar projections is mapped back onto the reference embryo surface (Figure S4) (Stower et al. 2023). vant is positive if there is net movement anteriorwards, and negative if there is net movement posteriorwards.

The corner points of each of the 640 ROI grids was propagated frame to frame using forwards Euler;


(i1,j1,t+1)=(i1,j1,t)+Flow(i1,j1,t)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left(i_{1}, j_{1}, t+1\right)=\left(i_{1}, j_{1}, t\right)+\operatorname{Flow}\left(i_{1}, j_{1}, t\right)$$\end{document}



where t is the frame index, until the end of the respective migration stage and Flow(i, j, t is the MOSES extracted DeepFlow optical flow from the VE and epiblast unwrapped 2D projection videos.

The mean fractional rate of change in surface area, dAdt\begin{document}$\frac{d A}{d t}$\end{document} of a local tissue patch was measured as previously reported (Stower et al. 2023) where dAdt\begin{document}$\frac{d A}{d t}$\end{document} at time t is defined as dAdt(t)=1ΔTA(t)−A(t−1)A(t−1)\begin{document}$\frac{d A}{d t}(t)=\frac{1}{\Delta T} \frac{A(t)-A(t-1)}{A(t-1)}$\end{document} where A = Area(t)/Area(t-1), and ΔT = 5 min for Lifeact-GFP and ΔT = 10 min for Hex-GFP:mem-tdTomato and Ttr-Cre:ROSA26mTmG embryos, the time elapsed between individual frames and dAdt(t)=0\begin{document}$\frac{d A}{d t}(t)=0$\end{document} for time t = 0. The control grid coordinates were mapped back to true Cartesian 3D coordinates and the 3D quadrilateral surface area at time t, was computed as
A(t)=12|(VB−VA)×(VC−VA)|+12|(VC−VA)×(VD−VA)| \begin{document}$$\displaystyle A(t)=\frac{1}{2}\left|\left(V_{B}-V_{A}\right) \times\left(V_{C}-V_{A}\right)\right|+\frac{1}{2}\left|\left(V_{C}-V_{A}\right) \times\left(V_{D}-V_{A}\right)\right|$$\end{document}
the sum of the area of the two constituent planar triangles △ ABC and △ ACD where VA, VB, VC, VD are the corresponding vertex (x, y, z) coordinates of the quadrilateral ABCD . For visualisation, we smoothed the dAdt\begin{document}$\frac{d A}{d t}$\end{document} for each of the 640 control grid tissue surface sectors temporally, running asymmetric least squares (Eilers and Boelens 2005) with parameters p = 0.5, λ = 100 for 10 iterations after edge-mode padding of signals by the maximum of T/100 or 3 frames.

Maintaining the same connectivity, this generates the corresponding 3D sector. For a 3D sector, we define the edge or link l, as the displacement between a pair of vertices, r1 = (x1, y1, z1) and r2 = (x2, y2, z2).
l=r2−r1=(X,Y,Z)=(x2−x1,y2−y1,z2−z1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle l =r_{2}-r_{1}=(X, Y, Z)=\left(x_{2}-x_{1}, y_{2}-y_{1}, z_{2}-z_{1}\right)$$\end{document}
The rate of change of the edge is the relative velocity between the two vertices, dldt=v(r2)−v(r1)\begin{document}$\frac{d l}{d t}=v\left(r_{2}\right)-v\left(r_{1}\right)$\end{document}.

The rate of change in link length can thus be expressed in terms of the velocity gradient, ∇v.
dldt≃∇vT⋅l \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{d l}{d t} \simeq \nabla v^{T} \cdot l$$\end{document}
This expression can be rearranged to give an expression for ∇v in terms of l by by taking the right outer product with l,
dldt⊗l≃(∇vT⋅l)⊗lCT=∇vTM∇v=M−1C \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{d l}{d t} \otimes l & \simeq\left(\nabla v^{T} \cdot l\right) \otimes l \\ C^{T} & =\nabla v^{T} M \\ \nabla v & =M^{-1} C\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where M=l⊗l,C=l⊗dldt\begin{document}$M=l \otimes l, C=l \otimes \frac{d l}{d t}$\end{document} and ⊗ denotes the tensor outer product. Letting W = M−1C, we proceed to decompose ∇v = W = Wsym + Wanti−sym uniquely into a symmetric, Wsym=12(W+WT)\begin{document}$W_{\mathrm{sym}}=\frac{1}{2}\left(W+W^{T}\right)$\end{document} and anti-symmetric component, Wanti-sym=12(W−WT)\begin{document}$W_{\text {anti-sym }}=\frac{1}{2}\left(W-W^{T}\right)$\end{document}.

The anti-symmetric component, Wanti−sym measures the local rotation rate of tissue motion or curl, Wcurl. In summary, we have the following complete decomposition of the velocity gradient.
∇v=W=(Wiso+Waniso)⏟Wsym+Wcurl⏟Wanti-sym \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \nabla v=W=\underbrace{\left(W_{\text {iso }}+W_{\text {aniso }}\right)}_{W_{\text {sym }}}+\underbrace{W_{\text {curl }}}_{W_{\text {anti-sym }}}$$\end{document}
In practice, the computations described were not implemented in Cartesian 3D. The sectors are local surface elements therefore for improved numerical stability we defined a local 2D surface coordinate system based on anterior-posterior and left-right directions. The edge vectors were projected with respect to this 2D basis which reduces the above computations to 2D. For visualisation, we smoothed the measured strain for each of the 640 control grid tissue surface patches temporally, running asymmetric least squares smoothing with parameters for 10 iterations after edge-mode padding of signals by the maximum of T/10 or 3 frames.

We model the tissue using a 2D vertex model (Alt et al. 2017; Fletcher et al. 2014) where each cell is represented as a polygon with vertices as the degrees of freedom. The equation of motion of the vertex is given by the following overdamped dynamics in the vertex model
ξr˙i=−∇riE+fiact \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \xi \dot{\mathbf{r}}_{i}=-\nabla_{\mathbf{r}_{i}} E+\mathbf{f}_{i}^{a c t}$$\end{document}
where ξ is the substrate friction coefficient and fiact\begin{document}$\mathbf{f}_{i}^{a c t}$\end{document} is an active force on vertex i. ∇ri\begin{document}$\nabla_{\mathbf{r}_{i}}$\end{document} is the gradient of the energy function E evaluated with respect to the position vector of vertex i. The energy function 45 is
E=∑c[KA2(Ac−A0(c))2+KP2(Pc−P0(c))2] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle E=\sum_{c}\left[\frac{K_{A}}{2}\left(A_{c}-A_{0}^{(c)}\right)^{2}+\frac{K_{P}}{2}\left(P_{c}-P_{0}^{(c)}\right)^{2}\right]$$\end{document}
where the summation is over all cells. Ac and Pc are the area and perimeter of cell c,A0(c)\begin{document}$c, A_{0}^{(c)}$\end{document} and P0(c)\begin{document}$P_{0}^{(c)}$\end{document} are the target area and perimeter for cell c, and KA and KP are the area and perimeter elasticity moduli. The quantity p0(c)=P0(c)/A0(c)\begin{document}$p_{0}^{(c)}=P_{0}^{(c)} / \sqrt{A_{0}^{(c)}}$\end{document} defines a target shape index. It is well known that a passive vertex model (with all cells having same target area and target perimeter) undergoes a rigidity transition near p0∗≈3.81\begin{document}$p_{0}^{*} \approx 3.81$\end{document} (Bi et al. 2015). Hence for p0<p0∗\begin{document}$p_{0}<p_{0}^{*}$\end{document} the tissue behaves like a solid whereas for p0>p0∗\begin{document}$p_{0}>p_{0}^{*}$\end{document} the tissue behaves like a fluid. We fix KA = 1 KP = 0.02 ξ = 1.

Equations of motion are solved using an explicit Euler scheme with time step 0.01. Edges that fall below a threshold length 0.01 undergo a T1 transition, with final length set to 0.011 (if an edge contains a ‘fixed’ vertex belonging to an exVE cell, it cannot undergo a T1). To add a self propulsion force on each DVE cell, we follow the prescription in (Sussman 2017). Each cell is assigned a self propulsion force αi. The additional active force on each vertex then reads
fjact=1nj∑injαini \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathbf{f}_{j}^{a c t}=\frac{1}{n_{j}} \sum_{i}^{n_{j}} \alpha_{i} \mathbf{n}_{i}$$\end{document}
where the sum is over all nj cells that share vertex j and ni = (0,1) is a unit vector along the direction of the polarity of cell . We set αi = 0 for the passive cells, whereas for the DVE cell αi goes linearly from 0 to F between t = 0 and t = 2000 and then remains constant.
